const body = `<h1>9.6a — Combatant Hierarchy: Interface + Creatures</h1>

<div class="callout warn">
    <strong>Season 2: beyond the exam.</strong> This topic is no longer tested
    (the 2025 CED removed inheritance from the exam). We build it after May 12
    because it makes the RPG engine dramatically better, and because you are
    ready for it. Pure craft, zero test anxiety.
</div>



<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">RPG Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>Your DungeonCrawl has one monster type at a time. But a real RPG has goblins,
    dragons, skeletons, slimes — each with different stats and behaviors. Inheritance
    makes this clean: one contract, many implementations.</p>

    <p>Open the <a href="../docs/unit-09/character-hierarchy.html">Combat System
    (Student RPG Guide)</a>. Follow Part 1: the class hierarchy.</p>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Build the Hierarchy</h3>

        <p><strong>Combatant</strong> interface:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><code>int attack()</code> — return damage dealt</li>
            <li><code>void takeDamage(int amount)</code> — subtract from HP</li>
            <li><code>boolean isAlive()</code> — HP > 0</li>
            <li><code>String getName()</code></li>
            <li><code>int getHp()</code></li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>Creature</strong> abstract class implements Combatant:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Protected fields: name, hp, maxHp, attackPower</li>
            <li>Constructor takes name, hp, attackPower</li>
            <li>Implements takeDamage(), isAlive(), getName(), getHp()</li>
            <li>Does NOT implement attack() — leave it abstract for subclasses</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>Concrete creatures:</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Goblin</strong> extends Creature — 20 HP, 5 ATK. attack() returns ATK + random 1-3.</li>
            <li><strong>Dragon</strong> extends Creature — 80 HP, 20 ATK. attack() returns random 15-25.</li>
            <li><strong>Skeleton</strong> extends Creature — 30 HP, 8 ATK. 30% chance to dodge: takeDamage() reduced to 0.</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>Player now implements Combatant.</strong> Your existing Player class
        already has attack(), takeDamage(), isAlive(), getName(). Add
        <code>implements Combatant</code> and a <code>getHp()</code> method.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Why is Creature abstract? What happens if you try
                <code>new Creature("???", 10, 5)</code>?</li>
            <li>Skeleton overrides takeDamage(), not attack(). If it has a 30% dodge
                chance, where does the random roll happen?</li>
            <li>Why does Dragon override attack() but not takeDamage()? What's the
                rule for which methods to override?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Acceptance — your hierarchy must:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Combatant interface with 5 method signatures</li>
            <li>Creature abstract — can't be instantiated directly</li>
            <li>Three creatures with distinct attack/takeDamage behavior</li>
            <li>Player implements Combatant with existing + new methods</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-09/character-hierarchy.html">Combat System (Student RPG Guide)</a></li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-09/extends-and-super.html">Inheritance Basics</a></li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-09/polymorphism.html">Polymorphism</a></li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
